#: src/pages/docs/concepts/CodecsPage.tsx:132
msgid "Conspect composites the canvas <0>once</0> per tick, encodes it <1>once per rendition</1>, and fans the same encoded packets out to every output that wants that rendition. Writing the wall to HLS while pushing it to an RTMP and an SRT destination at the same codec, resolution, and bitrate costs one encode — not three. A second encode is created only when an output genuinely needs a different codec, resolution, or bitrate."
msgstr ""

#: src/components/broadcast/BroadcastShell.tsx:39
msgid "CONSPECT Control — Desk"
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/DevicesPage.tsx:74
msgid "Conspect display node (displaynode)"
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/OverviewPage.tsx:35
msgid "Conspect is a live-media production, routing, and monitoring system. It brings together live sources, audio routing, compositing, overlays, playout, and output delivery. Multiview layouts and monitoring walls are part of that wider production workflow."
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/DevicesHelpPage.tsx:44
msgid "Conspect is designed to isolate program-output timing from managed-device failures. If a device fails, sources bound to it ride the normal tile ladder and outputs ride their failover policy — the multiview keeps running while the driver works on recovery."
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/concepts/LatencyPage.tsx:134
msgid "Conspect is designed to preserve output cadence under load by degrading the cheapest tiles first. This is a design objective, not a guarantee for every workload or host."
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/DataPage.tsx:151
msgid "Conspect is source-available, so a source build can compile the heartbeat client out. We state that plainly: removing it does not grant a commercial right — the licence terms bind regardless of the binary. The official builds carry the heartbeat for the smooth claim and lease experience."
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/OutputsPage.tsx:402
msgid "Conspect Program"
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/concepts/ColorPage.tsx:102
msgid "Conspect tone-maps HDR tiles into an SDR canvas (anchored so normal \"reference white\" content stays at a sensible brightness rather than scaling everything down), and only treats a stream as HDR when it is explicitly tagged as such — resolution alone never implies HDR. The canvas itself can also be configured as HDR (PQ or HLG) for monitoring HDR productions."
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/concepts/TimingSyncPage.tsx:29
msgid "Conspect's output is paced by exactly one thing: a fixed-cadence internal clock. At every tick — for example every 1/50 s for a 50 fps canvas — the invariant requires one finished frame. This is a design requirement, not an uptime warranty. Sources never set that pace. Each input writes its newest decoded frame into a per-tile store, and at each tick the compositor <0>samples</0> whatever is freshest there. A source that bursts, stalls, or dies changes only what its own tile shows — never when the next output frame goes out."
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/DevicesHelpPage.tsx:113
msgid "Conspect's own display-node software for single-board computers and spare PCs driving displays; enrolled nodes are located by their keypair identity rather than an address, and present frame-accurate synchronized output."
msgstr ""

#: src/docs/registry.ts:333
msgid "Conspect's own playout endpoints: enrolled, frame-accurate wall heads on small computers."
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/DisplayNodesHelpPage.tsx:18
msgid "Conspect's own playout endpoints: small computers that turn ordinary displays into managed, frame-accurate wall heads."
msgstr ""

