msgid "RTMP"
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/concepts/TransportsPage.tsx:130
msgid "RTMP is a TCP push protocol that remains the lingua franca for sending a stream <0>to</0> a service — most streaming platforms still ingest it. Classic RTMP carries H.264 video and AAC audio only; newer \"enhanced\" endpoints negotiate H.265 and AV1. As an input it appears when an encoder or app pushes to Conspect; as an output it is how Conspect pushes the composed canvas to a platform."
msgstr ""

#: src/docs/registry.ts:393
#: src/docs/registry.ts:728
#: src/pages/docs/concepts/GlossaryPage.tsx:267
#: src/pages/docs/concepts/TransportsPage.tsx:54
#: src/pages/docs/concepts/TransportsPage.tsx:275
msgid "RTSP"
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/concepts/TransportsPage.tsx:56
msgid "RTSP (Real Time Streaming Protocol) is the standard way IP cameras and encoders publish a live stream on a local network. The client asks the device to describe and play its stream; the media itself then flows as RTP packets, either over UDP or interleaved in the same TCP connection. Latency is low (commonly a few hundred milliseconds) and the URL form is simple: <0>rtsp://camera.local:8554/main</0>."
msgstr ""

#: src/pages/docs/concepts/TransportsPage.tsx:67
msgid "RTSP carries whatever the camera encodes — usually H.264 or H.265 — so the picture quality and bitrate are set at the camera. It has no built-in recovery beyond TCP retransmission; on flaky links a stream simply stalls and must reconnect, which Conspect handles per tile without disturbing the rest of the wall."
msgstr ""

